        explanation:
          "Now, turn to whether a classified offense actually reaches one of these courts at all. Unlike Germany's Legalitätsprinzip, which imposes a general duty to prosecute once sufficient evidence exists, French law follows the principe de l'opportunité des poursuites, the principle of prosecutorial discretion: Article 40-1 of the Code de procédure pénale gives the Procureur de la République genuine discretion to decide whether prosecution is actually appropriate, and the prosecutor can classer sans suite, close the matter without further action, even where the evidence would clearly support a charge, if prosecution is not, in the prosecutor's own judgment, the appropriate response given the circumstances [5].",
