    connections:
      "Ten ideas, one recurring comparison: where German law mandates prosecution once there is sufficient evidence, ask what discretion French law actually gives its own prosecutors instead. The three-element analysis is the analytical backbone for any offense, and the contraventions, délits, and crimes classification mechanically determines which court and procedure apply. L'opportunité des poursuites shows how much discretion a French prosecutor actually holds over whether a classified case ever reaches a court at all, and détention provisoire, decided by the institutionally separate JLD, and garde à vue protections govern how a suspect is held and questioned before trial. The CRPC resolves many cases through a negotiated, judge-approved guilty plea without ever reaching a full trial, while the juge d'instruction directs the considerably more involved investigation the genuinely serious and complex cases actually require. The cour d'assises jury is where the most serious of those cases, crimes, are ultimately tried, and la partie civile gives the victim their own distinctive role and remedy within whichever track the case actually follows. Présomption d'innocence, finally, runs underneath every one of these nine ideas at once, a codified guarantee with its own specific civil-law enforcement mechanism. As you read any French criminal case from here forward, try running it through all ten ideas in order, and resist the reflex to assume a German or common-law rule, mandatory prosecution, an American-style plea bargain, transfers automatically to this system.",
